ay: 2.58\n\nI'll compute var for these six months in 1944 to see how the method works:\n(2.65+2.65+2.64+2.61+2.58+2.58)/6 ≈ 2.62 mean\n\nI see the pattern continues with June's data at 2.58 for the first half of 1944. From here I'll do the exact variance compute to verify the method: values=[2.65,2.65,2.64,2.61,2.58,2.58]\nMean ≈ 2.618333333\nvariance = sum((x-mean)^2)/n ≈\n\nI'm exploring potential interpretations of the variance calculation. If the task is referring to sample variance with a divisor of (n-1) = 5, the result would be different. There are multiple computational approaches to consider: using a divisor of n or (n-1), each producing distinctly different outcomes.\n","signature":""},{"type":"toolRequest","id":"call_function_x3feqylem6xx_1","toolCall":{"status":"success","value":{"name":"shell","arguments":{"command":"python3 << 'EOF'\n# Let me check if perhaps the issue is that there's actually a different parsing we should do \n# for the table - maybe the years in the table structure could have 1938 in a column we haven't correctly parsed  \n# Let me re-parse and check all columns available in the monthly structure correctly this time \n\nimport re\n\nwith open('/app/resources/treasury_bulletin_1947_02_page_87.txt', 'r') as f:\n    content = f.read()\n\n# Remove newlines to simplify parsing  \ncontent = content.replace('\\n', '')\n\n# Find the main table after \"Monthly series\"\nmatch = re.search(r'Monthly series.*?<table>(.*?)</table>', content, re.DOTALL)\nif match:\n    table = match.group(1)\n    \n    # Now let's parse each row properly\n    rows = table.split('</tr><tr>')\n    \n    # Each data row has 9 cells (<td>...</td><td>...</td>...) of format:\n    # Date1 | PartiallyTaxExempt1 | Taxable1 | Date2 | PartiallyTaxExempt2 | Taxable2 | Date3 | PartiallyTaxExempt3 | Taxable3 | Corporate3\n    \n    # Parse row-by-row to build complete list  \n    all_data = {}\n    current_row_num = 0\n    for row in rows:\n        cells = re.findall(r'<td>([^<